ction, modelled on the kimi lane. It sets `HOME` to an empty scratch dir and sets `CODEX_HOME` to a scratch `.codex` inside it, so the real `~/.codex` is never opened. The scratch dir is removed afterwards. The `codex` and `kimi` lanes are unchanged.\n- **`README.md`**: lane list entry for `codex-sandbox`.\n- **`ANSWER.md`**: full write-up of the change, the checks, and the open points.\n- **`sandbox/agent.sb`**: unchanged. Its existing rules already cover the real home.\n\n**Verification**\n- `bash -n run.sh` passes.\n- Under the profile, reading `~/.codex/config.toml` and creating a file in `~` are both denied.\n- `codex --version` starts under the profile with the scratch `HOME` and `CODEX_HOME`.\n- A stub `codex` run through `./run.sh fizz codex-sandbox 9` produced `meta.json`, `final.txt`, and `answer.json`. The stub's read of the real `auth.json` was denied. I deleted that test run folder afterwards.\n\n**Gap: authentication**\n- The README says Codex logs in from `CODEX_API_KEY`, and that variable is not set in this shell. The only credential here is `~/.codex/auth.json`, which the sandbox blocks by design. A real `codex-sandbox` run will fail at login until the key is exported.\n- I did not copy `auth.json` into the scratch dir. That would give the agent's shell the real credential.\n\nI did not run a real Codex task, since that would send the fizz prompt to OpenAI and you asked for the lane, not an eval run. To try it for real, export `CODEX_API_KEY` and run `./run.sh fizz codex-sandbox 1`.","ttft_ms":2313,"type":"result","duration_ms":63119,"uuid":"c9d1e0ae-1b75-4bcb-a18f-2c6c276eac1f","ttft_stream_ms":2108,"time_to_request_ms":275,"first_content_frame_ms":2108,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":20,"outputTokens":10710,"cacheReadInputTokens":299006,"cacheCreationInputTokens":22867,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.01292046,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens